WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Install and packaging rules define what leaves the build system.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.200
That makes them security-relevant even when the compiled code is correct.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:21.100
A package can include test utilities, local configuration, temporary files, private development data, stale generated assets, extra libraries, or debug material that was never intended for users.

4
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.960
Artifact hygiene is the discipline of knowing exactly what was produced.

5
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.360
RPATH and runtime search behavior deserve special attention.

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.500
They influence how an installed program finds shared libraries after it is deployed.

7
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.520
The right policy depends on platform and distribution model, but the project should not leave the result to chance.

8
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:47.860
Reviewers should know which directories are encoded, which paths are removed for release, and how package layout supports the expected runtime behavior.

9
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.100
Packaging metadata is part of the product contract.

10
00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:01.120
Names, versions, licenses, dependencies, component selections, symbols, debug files, and archive contents should match policy and documentation.

11
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:11.700
Debug symbols may be valuable for support and incident response, but they often belong in a separate controlled artifact rather than inside the user-facing package.

12
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.840
Diagnostic outputs should be treated with the same care.

13
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.080
Before delivery, release artifacts should be inspected.

14
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:29.780
Teams can list package contents, check linked libraries, confirm signing steps where applicable, compare files against expected manifests, and preserve build logs for traceability.

15
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:39.160
CMake should make those checks easier by keeping install rules narrow, component names clear, output directories predictable, and packaging steps repeatable in CI.
